  "verdict": "Choose Outlier if you want the closest direct alternative to DataAnnotation's project workflow, provided you are comfortable with rates that adjust per task. If you hold an advanced credential such as a law degree, medical license, or PhD, apply to Mercor or Handshake AI, where published and reported hourly rates frequently exceed $70 to $100 per hour. For generalist writers seeking published entry benchmarks without advanced degrees, Mindrift provides the clearest baseline at $15 to $30 per hour, while Prolific guarantees an $8 hourly floor for research-backed studies.\n\nOur recommendation would change if legal proceedings alter independent contractor operations across these platforms. Scale AI's $12.5 million settlement in California worker misclassification lawsuits and Handshake's unpaid wage litigation under the Fair Labor Standards Act indicate rising regulatory pressure on task classifications. If platforms transition from flexible 1099 contracts to structured agency employment, project flexibility and onboarding standards will shift.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How These Platforms Differ From DataAnnotation",
      "content": "DataAnnotation structures its work around a Starter Assessment, paying contractors starting at $25 to $50 per hour for general tasks and $40 to $150 per hour for coding projects via PayPal. Its alternatives differ primarily in their hiring standards, pay transparency, and client contracts.\n\n Mercor distributes pay weekly on Wednesdays via Stripe Connect or Wise, rather than using PayPal withdrawals. Outlier, operated by Scale AI, uses an automated tasking queue similar to DataAnnotation but requires an associate degree, resume, and LinkedIn profile during onboarding.\n\nWe excluded two older crowdsourcing providers from this roundup. TELUS Digital does not publish verified rate cards or payment mechanics on an official worker page, and Remotasks halted new signups across several international markets while its current status remains unconfirmed. The six platforms included here maintain verifiable, active worker portals as of October 2026."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Which Alternative Fits Your Background",
      "content": "Your academic background and professional credentials determine which platform yields the highest return on your tasking time. AI labs pay wide rate spreads depending on whether a task requires basic natural language fluency or specialized technical domain knowledge.\n\nGeneralist writers without specialized degrees should start with Mindrift or Outlier. Mindrift publishes baseline rates of $15 to $30 per hour for entry-level tasks, while Outlier offers prompt-ranking projects that mirror generalist work on DataAnnotation. Prolific also serves generalist contributors well by guaranteeing a minimum researcher wage of $8 per hour, with a recommended benchmark of $12 per hour.\n\nCoders and STEM specialists should focus on Alignerr, Handshake AI, or Mercor. Handshake AI publishes rates from $30 to $125 per hour, though its fellowship primarily recruits Master's, PhD, and Postdoc candidates. Mercor connects scientists, doctors, and lawyers directly to frontier AI labs, reporting average expert earnings above $85 per hour and individual job listings paying up to $110 per hour for news analysts and journalists."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Red Flags When You Search for AI Training Work",
      "content": "Legitimate AI training platforms never charge workers signup fees, equipment costs, or assessment testing fees. The Federal Trade Commission issued a consumer alert warning job seekers about remote work scams where fraudulent recruiters contact candidates via text message or messaging apps offering online assessor jobs.\n\nThe FTC notes that scam operations frequently ask victims to deposit money to unlock higher task earnings or send fraudulent checks. Both DataAnnotation and Mindrift state on their official websites that joining, assessments, and onboarding tests are completely free. If an entity posing as an AI annotation platform asks you to deposit money or pay fees, report the encounter to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov immediately."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Taxes on AI Training Income",
      "content": "Earnings from AI training platforms represent taxable self-employment income that must be reported to the Internal Revenue Service regardless of whether you receive a tax form. When you complete tasks on platforms like Outlier, Mindrift, or DataAnnotation, you operate as an independent contractor rather than an employee.\n\nPlatforms do not withhold federal income tax, Social Security, or Medicare from your task payouts. Mercor notes in its tax policies that US workers submitting a Form W-9 receive Form 1099-NEC if earnings exceed $600 in a calendar year. You can calculate your federal self-employment tax burden and quarterly obligations using our [1099 tax calculator](/self-employment-tax/1099-tax-calculator/) or explore detailed reporting strategies in our guide to [DataAnnotation taxes](/self-employment-tax/dataannotation-taxes/)."
    }
  ],
